WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:19.210
The organisms we highlight on this channel
are all aquatic, they must be submerged in

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.990
water in order to thrive.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:30.690
Residing in ponds, swamps, rivers, and oceans,
but also places that we don’t think of as

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:34.050
bodies of water...places that are simply wet.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:39.770
When you’re a microbe, the thin film of
water on a snail’s back, or clinging to

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:45.820
moss on a tree, can be a deep well of that
good stuff.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:54.180
Of course, we humans are also dependent on
water, in a sense...our cells are aquatic.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.730
We carry our rivers inside of us.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.840
And to fill our internal aquatic environment,
we drink.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:08.570
We also bathe in water, we cook with it, clean
with it, flush with it, and we make things with it.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:15.940
And when we’re done with water, the result
of our use of it is something we call wastewater:

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:25.469
a mix of sewage, storm runoff, and other water
that has, in some way, come in contact with us.

00:01:25.469 --> 00:01:32.299
And, you know, less idyllic than your local
pond, wastewater is nonetheless a promising

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:35.060
habitat for many a microbe.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:41.770
That’s part of what makes wastewater dangerous,
both for us and the environment.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:48.969
Diseases born out of bacteria, viruses, and
parasites can thrive in wastewater, which

00:01:48.969 --> 00:01:55.079
may also be filled with pollutants like metals
or the ingredients of our own pharmaceuticals

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:56.590
and toiletries.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:02.959
And should the wastewater come in contact
with different ecosystems, the combined decay

00:02:02.959 --> 00:02:10.179
of organic matter and excess of nutrients
can create a chain of events that depletes

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:18.030
oxygen from the water and affects the balance
of life previously maintained there.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:26.480
Dealing with wastewater has inspired engineering
across civilizations, from Mesopotamian stormwater

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:32.240
drains to Mohenjo-daro latrines to Roman sewer
systems.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:39.420
Those have all built up towards our modern
wastewater treatment systems that clean the

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:47.190
water, a feat of engineering that is nonetheless
built on microbial ecosystems whose history

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:53.230
is much, much more ancient than our own existence.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:58.800
Bodies of water have to deal with pollutants,
whether they be the product of local shifts

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:05.530
in nature, death and decay, or just plain
old fashioned feces.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:14.040
To us, these bits of organic matter are contaminants,
but to microbes, it’s a veritable buffet,

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.690
leading to a process called self-purification.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:25.760
Bacteria, coming from the water or the waste
itself, shape this self-purification, consuming

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:32.020
organic and inorganic matter while producing
compounds that benefit their neighbors.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:36.490
As they eat, the bacteria grow and divide.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:43.140
But just because this water is full of waste
doesn’t mean that the bacteria within it

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:50.250
is immune from the drama of the food chain
that dominates nature at all scales.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:57.290
The waste-consuming bacteria become plentiful,
creating an attraction for various protozoa

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:00.850
that have a range of dining habits.

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:08.520
Peritrich ciliates, like Vorticella and Epistylis,
use a ring of cilia around their mouths to

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:15.540
create tiny vortexes in the water, allowing
them to draw in and consume their bacterial

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.170
prey rapidly.

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:27.340
You might also find amoebas dining on bacteria,
crawling and increasing in size and number.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:34.000
These protozoa themselves then become the
prey of multicellular organisms like rotifers,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:40.330
water bears, and nematodes, and the excretions
of those organisms provide waste that further

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.320
feeds the bacteria whose excretions provide
waste that further feed the bacteria.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:50.200
The consuming done by unicellular and multicellular
organisms helps clear the water, creating

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:55.600
an ecosystem that doesn’t just feed, it
cleans.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:04.980
We are then living in a world that has its
own built in wastewater treatment system,

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:12.520
but not necessarily one constructed to handle
the large volumes of wastewater created by

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:18.880
dense gatherings of people, whether in the
form of towns, cities, or industries.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:27.350
And so we’ve created sewage treatment plants
that are, at their core, these same ecosystems

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:34.880
recapitulated in an industrial setting, with
reactors full of microbes consuming and being

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:39.700
consumed as the particulates of waste are
eaten and broken down.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:45.710
The primary difference between how this works
in nature and how it works in reactors is

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:51.960
that we can control the reactor, tuning the
temperature and oxygen levels to keep the

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:56.820
microbes happy and cleaning.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:03.160
One of the common methods used in wastewater
treatment is called activated sludge, which

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:09.960
is sludge, but alive with bacteria, fungi,
and protozoa.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:19.240
When mixed with wastewater, that sludge becomes
a version of the self-purifying ecosystem from nature.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:27.120
The microbes consume the waste or each other,
clumping together into particles called flocs

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:34.970
that settle to the bottom as a new mass of
activated sludge, with clearer water floating above.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:41.600
The activated sludge can be removed and potentially
even reused, while the cleaner water goes

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:46.840
through more cleaning to be ready for release
back into the environment

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:53.840
There are so many ways that we have learned
from or taken inspiration from the world around

00:06:53.840 --> 00:07:00.560
us, whether it’s the colors, the sounds,
or the creatures who spark our imaginations.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.710
But wastewater treatment is probably one of
the most important.

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:13.669
Indeed, wastewater treatment is the largest
industrial, reactor-based use of microbes,

00:07:13.669 --> 00:07:17.600
even beyond brewing or baking.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:24.600
One recent study analyzing about 1,200 samples
of activated sludge from 23 countries found

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:32.540
that there about 1 billion types of bacteria
represented around the world, with only a

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:40.430
fraction of a fraction of a fraction of a
percent representing species that we have

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:43.250
even begun to study.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:51.199
While not a particularly glamorous job, our
modern towns and cities are built on sludge

00:07:51.199 --> 00:07:58.320
and microbes that, by just living their own
lives and eating what looks good to them,

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.540
make our clean water possible.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:10.140
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:13.360
And thank you as well to all of our patrons on Patreon.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:18.400
We are tremendously grateful that we can share our enthusiasm for

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:23.160
microbes and also wastewater treatment with the world,

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:26.100
and our patrons are the reasons why.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:29.400
if you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes James

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.900
Check out Jam and Germs on Instagram

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:38.100
And if you want to see more from us, there's probably a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

